QUESTIONS.push(
{"id": "TBL3-001", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-apap-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Acetaminophen'", "stem": "Which entry gives the symptoms and diagnostic method for acute acetaminophen poisoning?", "options": [{"t": "Nausea/vomiting and signs of liver failure; blood levels can be measured", "correct": true, "why": "Acetaminophen is metabolized to NAPQI, a free radical that damages the liver, so the row lists clinical signs of liver failure along with nausea/vomiting. Acetaminophen is common enough in overdose that blood levels can be measured to confirm it."}, {"t": "CNS depression and relatively normal vitals; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "That is the benzodiazepines row."}, {"t": "Bradycardia, arrhythmias, high serum potassium; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "That is the digoxin row (bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, nausea/vomiting)."}, {"t": "Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "Those are tricyclic antidepressant symptoms; the key lists no blood-level diagnostic method for that row."}], "teach": "Acetaminophen's harm comes from its metabolite NAPQI, a free radical that damages the liver; that is why the row lists clinical signs of liver failure along with nausea/vomiting. Acetaminophen is one of the compounds so common in overdose, and detectable in blood, that emergency rooms check for it, so a blood level can confirm the poisoning. The antidote, N-acetylcysteine, replenishes the glutathione that conjugates NAPQI.", "quote": "any other compounds that it's likely emergency rooms would test a person's blood for to see if they've been exposed to them? ... acetaminophen. Salicylates Iron Beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, and there's a few others, but those are some of the big ones ... opiates as well", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Acetaminophen’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-002", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-apap-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Acetaminophen'", "stem": "What is the antidote for acetaminophen overdose?", "options": [{"t": "N-acetylcysteine", "correct": true, "why": "Acetaminophen's toxic metabolite NAPQI is conjugated by glutathione. N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione by supplying ingredients to make more, so NAPQI keeps being cleared."}, {"t": "Fomepizole", "correct": false, "why": "Fomepizole (or ethanol) is the toxic alcohols antidote."}, {"t": "Deferoxamine", "correct": false, "why": "Deferoxamine is the iron antidote."}, {"t": "Sodium thiosulfate", "correct": false, "why": "Sodium thiosulfate is given for cyanide poisoning; like N-acetylcysteine it enhances metabolism (it supplies sulfur to the body's enzyme rhodanese), but it is not the acetaminophen antidote."}], "teach": "N-acetylcysteine (NAC) is the acetaminophen antidote. On 10/19 she placed it under enhancing metabolism: NAC is given as a drug and supplies ingredients to make more glutathione, which conjugates the toxic metabolite NAPQI.", "quote": "anastylcysteine, we give it as a drug. And it's giving ingredients to the system to create more glutathione.", "note": "The transcript renders N-acetylcysteine as \"anastylcysteine\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Acetaminophen’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-003", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-apap-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Acetaminophen'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of N-acetylcysteine (NAC) in acetaminophen overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Replenishes glutathione", "correct": true, "why": "The key's mechanism column: replenishes glutathione. On 10/19 she said NAC supplies ingredients to make more glutathione."}, {"t": "Induces the enzymes that metabolize acetaminophen", "correct": false, "why": "Enzyme induction takes too much time to treat a poisoning; the deck says cofactors are given instead. N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione."}, {"t": "Inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase", "correct": false, "why": "That is ethanol or fomepizole, for the toxic alcohols."}, {"t": "Chelates the circulating ferric iron", "correct": false, "why": "That is deferoxamine, for iron."}], "teach": "Her key: N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione. Glutathione conjugates NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen; in overdose the glutathione runs short, and NAC provides ingredients to make more. On 10/19 she classed this as enhancing metabolism by giving a cofactor.", "quote": "anastylcysteine, we give it as a drug. And it's giving ingredients to the system to create more glutathione.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Acetaminophen’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-019"},
{"id": "TBL3-004", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "apply", "concept": "tbl-apap-8h", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Acetaminophen', special considerations column", "stem": "At what time after an acetaminophen overdose does the antidote lose effectiveness?", "options": [{"t": "About 8 hours after overdose", "correct": true, "why": "Her key's time point is about 8 hours. NAC acts by replenishing the glutathione that conjugates NAPQI; the key says it loses effectiveness after about 8 hours, yet it is still given past that point."}, {"t": "About 2 hours after overdose", "correct": false, "why": "About 2 hours is the single-dose activated charcoal window as she said it in lecture (her Quiz 3 key says less than 60 minutes), not the N-acetylcysteine time point."}, {"t": "About 24 hours after overdose", "correct": false, "why": "24 hours is the out-of-hospital window for modified-release verapamil, not the NAC time point, which is about 8 hours."}, {"t": "About 48 hours after overdose", "correct": false, "why": "No 48-hour point appears in the acetaminophen row; the key's point is about 8 hours, and even after that NAC is still given."}], "teach": "N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione, which conjugates NAPQI, the liver-damaging metabolite of acetaminophen. The special-considerations question asks when NAC loses effectiveness: about 8 hours after the overdose. Its second half asks whether to give NAC after that point, and the key says yes: losing effectiveness is not the same as having no effect. The 8 hours is separate from the 2-hour window for single-dose charcoal.", "note": "The 8-hour point is in her key only; it was not stated aloud on 10/19 or 10/20. Keyed on her key, which she said sets the detail expected.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Acetaminophen’"},
{"id": "TBL3-005", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "apply", "concept": "tbl-apap-still-give", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Acetaminophen', special considerations column", "stem": "A patient arrives 12 hours after an acetaminophen overdose, past the time point when N-acetylcysteine loses effectiveness. Should it still be given?", "options": [{"t": "Yes; N-acetylcysteine is still given", "correct": true, "why": "Past about 8 hours NAC is less effective, not useless: it still replenishes the glutathione that conjugates NAPQI, so her key says to give it."}, {"t": "No; it no longer works past 8 hours", "correct": false, "why": "Her key says it loses effectiveness, not all effect, and answers yes: still give it."}, {"t": "No; give fomepizole instead", "correct": false, "why": "Fomepizole is the toxic alcohol antidote (it inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase). For acetaminophen her key says yes, still give N-acetylcysteine."}, {"t": "No; give supportive care instead", "correct": false, "why": "Supportive care is the salicylate and tricyclic antidepressant approach. For acetaminophen her key says yes, still give N-acetylcysteine."}], "teach": "The acetaminophen special-considerations question has two parts. The antidote loses effectiveness about 8 hours after overdose, and it should still be given past that time point. Losing effectiveness is not the same as having no effect.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Acetaminophen’"},
{"id": "TBL3-006", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-methanol-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Toxic alcohols'", "stem": "Which findings do the active metabolites of methanol cause?", "options": [{"t": "Anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity", "correct": true, "why": "The toxic alcohols row: methanol active metabolites can cause anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity. On 10/19 she named formic acid, which damages the retina."}, {"t": "Crystals in the microvasculature; renal toxicity", "correct": false, "why": "That is ethylene glycol, the other toxic alcohol."}, {"t": "Elevated serum potassium and arrhythmias", "correct": false, "why": "That is digoxin."}, {"t": "Respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis", "correct": false, "why": "That is salicylates."}], "teach": "Alcohol dehydrogenase turns methanol into formaldehyde, and formaldehyde dehydrogenase turns that into formic acid. These active metabolites, not methanol itself, do the harm: the slide pairs the methanol pathway with metabolic acidosis and tissue injury, and formic acid damages the retina, which is the ocular toxicity and loss of sight. Blocking alcohol dehydrogenase with ethanol or fomepizole keeps the metabolites from forming.", "quote": "Formic acid will cause damage, especially to the eyes, to the retina of the eyes.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Toxic alcohols’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-007", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-eg-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Toxic alcohols'", "stem": "Which finding do the active metabolites of ethylene glycol cause?", "options": [{"t": "Crystals in the microvasculature and renal toxicity", "correct": true, "why": "Ethylene glycol is metabolized through glycolic acid to oxalic acid, which can precipitate as microcrystals in small vessels; the blocked blood flow damages organs, and the kidney is the common target."}, {"t": "Anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity", "correct": false, "why": "That is methanol, the other toxic alcohol."}, {"t": "Liver failure after nausea and vomiting", "correct": false, "why": "That is acetaminophen."}, {"t": "Hyperthermia, confusion and seizures", "correct": false, "why": "That is the salicylates row."}], "teach": "Her key: ethylene glycol active metabolites can crystallize in the microvasculature, and renal toxicity is common. On 10/19 she named the metabolite oxalic acid, which forms microcrystals that block blood flow in small vessels and damage organs.", "quote": "sometimes the oxalic acid can form into little crystals and blocks blood flow and damages those organs.", "note": "The key spells it \"crystalize\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Toxic alcohols’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-008", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-alc-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Toxic alcohols'", "stem": "What are the antidotes for toxic alcohol (methanol or ethylene glycol) poisoning?", "options": [{"t": "Ethanol or fomepizole", "correct": true, "why": "Methanol and ethylene glycol are both turned into their toxic metabolites by alcohol dehydrogenase. Ethanol outcompetes that enzyme and fomepizole binds and inhibits it, so one antidote pair serves both."}, {"t": "Atropine or pralidoxime", "correct": false, "why": "Atropine and pralidoxime are the organophosphate insecticide antidotes."}, {"t": "Calcium or glucagon", "correct": false, "why": "Calcium and glucagon are on the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker list."}, {"t": "Naloxone or flumazenil", "correct": false, "why": "Naloxone is the opioid antidote; flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote."}], "teach": "Methanol and ethylene glycol do their harm only after alcohol dehydrogenase starts converting them: methanol on to formic acid, ethylene glycol on to oxalic acid. Blocking that shared first enzyme stops both toxic pathways, so the two poisons share an antidote: ethanol, which competes for the enzyme, or fomepizole, which binds and inhibits it. This is the inhibit-bioactivation treatment.", "quote": "you can give ethanol. And outcompete the alcohol ... dehydrogenase metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol. Same thing with fomeprazole. It'll ... bind to and inhibit the alcohol dehydrogenase and prevent the metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol.", "note": "The transcript renders fomepizole as \"fomeprazole\" and \"omeprazole\"; omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor, so this is a transcription error (see L16-008).", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Toxic alcohols’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-009", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-alc-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Toxic alcohols'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of ethanol and fomepizole in toxic alcohol poisoning?", "options": [{"t": "Inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase", "correct": true, "why": "Ethanol competes with methanol or ethylene glycol for alcohol dehydrogenase, and fomepizole binds and inhibits the enzyme; either way the conversion to formic acid or oxalic acid is blocked."}, {"t": "Replenishes glutathione", "correct": false, "why": "That is N-acetylcysteine."}, {"t": "Renal ion trapping to enhance elimination", "correct": false, "why": "That is sodium bicarbonate for salicylates."}, {"t": "Chelates the circulating ferric iron", "correct": false, "why": "That is deferoxamine."}], "teach": "Ethanol and fomepizole both inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase, the enzyme that starts the conversion of methanol and ethylene glycol to their toxic metabolites. On 10/19 she said ethanol outcompetes the enzyme and fomepizole binds to and inhibits it. This is the inhibit-bioactivation treatment.", "quote": "you can give ethanol. And outcompete the alcohol ... dehydrogenase metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol. Same thing with fomeprazole. It'll ... bind to and inhibit the alcohol dehydrogenase and prevent the metabolism of methanol or ethylene glycol.", "note": "The transcript renders fomepizole as \"fomeprazole\" and \"omeprazole\"; omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor, so this is a transcription error (see L16-008).", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Toxic alcohols’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-008"},
{"id": "TBL3-010", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "apply", "concept": "tbl-alc-advantage", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Toxic alcohols', special considerations column", "stem": "Toxic alcohol poisoning has two possible treatments. Which one has the advantage, and why?", "options": [{"t": "Fomepizole: stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, fewer adverse effects", "correct": true, "why": "Her key: fomepizole is a stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects than ethanol. On 10/19 she added that it gives better control of the dosing."}, {"t": "Ethanol: stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, fewer adverse effects", "correct": false, "why": "Ethanol is the older option, the Everclear shot she called unrefined. Her key gives fomepizole the edge: the stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, with fewer adverse effects."}, {"t": "Fomepizole: stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, but more adverse effects", "correct": false, "why": "Half right: fomepizole is the stronger inhibitor, and it also has fewer adverse effects than ethanol."}, {"t": "Neither: the two are equal in strength and in adverse effects", "correct": false, "why": "The two are not equal. Both block alcohol dehydrogenase, but fomepizole blocks it more strongly, causes fewer adverse effects, and gives better control of the dose than shots of ethanol."}], "teach": "Her key answers the special-considerations question: fomepizole is the stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects than ethanol. On 10/19 she described emergency rooms that once kept Everclear for a shot or two of ethanol, and said fomepizole gives better control of the dose.", "quote": "We now have ... the medication omeprazole that we'll give to patients. ... a little bit better control of the dosing.", "note": "The transcript renders fomepizole as \"fomeprazole\" and \"omeprazole\"; omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor, so this is a transcription error (see L16-008). L16-009 keys the dose-control point from her speech; the key adds strength and adverse effects.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Toxic alcohols’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-011", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-benzo-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Benzodiazepines'", "stem": "Which entry gives the symptoms and diagnostic method for benzodiazepine overdose?", "options": [{"t": "CNS depression and relatively normal vitals; blood levels can be measured", "correct": true, "why": "Benzodiazepines agonize the GABA receptor, and the overdose shows as CNS depression while the vital signs stay relatively normal. Benzodiazepine levels can be measured in the blood to confirm."}, {"t": "Nausea/vomiting and signs of liver failure; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "That is the acetaminophen row."}, {"t": "Bradycardia, arrhythmias, high serum potassium; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "That is the digoxin row (bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, nausea/vomiting)."}, {"t": "Acetylcholine excess (SLUDGE or DUMBELLS); blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "Acetylcholine excess is the organophosphate insecticide row; the key lists no blood-level diagnostic method for it."}], "teach": "Her key: benzodiazepine overdose shows CNS depression with relatively normal vital signs, and benzodiazepine levels can be measured in the blood. The normal vitals separate it from the opioid picture, where heart and breathing rates fall.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Benzodiazepines’"},
{"id": "TBL3-012", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-benzo-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Benzodiazepines'", "stem": "What is the antidote for benzodiazepine overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Flumazenil", "correct": true, "why": "The benzodiazepine is agonizing its receptor; flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at that receptor, so it blocks the overdosed drug's effect."}, {"t": "Naloxone", "correct": false, "why": "Naloxone is the opioid antidote."}, {"t": "Atropine", "correct": false, "why": "Atropine is in the beta blocker and organophosphate rows."}, {"t": "Physostigmine", "correct": false, "why": "Physostigmine was the 10/19 example for belladonna alkaloids; it is not in the table."}], "teach": "Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote. On 10/19 she gave it as an example of a treatment that is a receptor antagonist: the benzodiazepine agonizes the receptor and flumazenil antagonizes it.", "quote": "Flonazanil is used commonly for ... overdoses of things like your benzodiazepines.", "note": "The transcript renders flumazenil as \"Flonazanil\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Benzodiazepines’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-013", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-benzo-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Benzodiazepines'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of flumazenil?", "options": [{"t": "Competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor", "correct": true, "why": "The overdosed benzodiazepine agonizes the GABA (benzodiazepine) receptor; flumazenil competes for that same receptor and blocks it, which is why it is listed as a competitive antagonist."}, {"t": "Competitive antagonist of opioid receptors", "correct": false, "why": "That is naloxone."}, {"t": "Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist", "correct": false, "why": "That is atropine, in the organophosphate row."}, {"t": "Agonist at the benzodiazepine receptor", "correct": false, "why": "The benzodiazepine is the agonist; flumazenil is the antagonist."}], "teach": "Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine receptor. The benzodiazepine in overdose is the agonist; the antidote competes with it at the same receptor. Naloxone does the same thing at the opioid receptor.", "quote": "Flonazanil is used commonly for ... overdoses of things like your benzodiazepines.", "note": "The transcript renders flumazenil as \"Flonazanil\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Benzodiazepines’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-041"},
{"id": "TBL3-014", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "apply", "concept": "tbl-benzo-concern", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Benzodiazepines', special considerations column", "stem": "What are some concerns when choosing to use flumazenil?", "options": [{"t": "It can cause withdrawal in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient", "correct": true, "why": "Flumazenil blocks the benzodiazepine receptor. In a patient dependent on benzodiazepines, blocking the drug's action at its receptor can bring on withdrawal symptoms."}, {"t": "It loses effectiveness about 8 hours after the overdose", "correct": false, "why": "That is the acetaminophen antidote's time point."}, {"t": "It makes later serum drug levels unhelpful for dosing", "correct": false, "why": "That is the digoxin antibody fragments special consideration."}, {"t": "It is a weaker inhibitor with more adverse effects", "correct": false, "why": "That describes ethanol compared with fomepizole."}], "teach": "Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist: it blocks the benzodiazepine receptor that the overdosed drug is agonizing. That reverses an overdose, but in a patient who is dependent on benzodiazepines, blocking the drug's action can cause withdrawal symptoms. Naloxone carries the parallel concern for the same reason: it blocks the opioid receptor and can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Benzodiazepines’"},
{"id": "TBL3-015", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-bb-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "stem": "Which entry gives the symptoms of beta-blocker overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status, respiratory depression", "correct": true, "why": "Beta-blocker overdose drops the blood pressure and slows the heart; the row adds altered mental status and respiratory depression, and the altered mental status is what her key uses to tell it from calcium channel blocker overdose."}, {"t": "Hypotension, bradycardia, but often alert", "correct": false, "why": "That is calcium channel blockers; the alert mental status is the difference."}, {"t": "Bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, nausea/vomiting", "correct": false, "why": "That is the digoxin row."}, {"t": "Somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, decreased pupils", "correct": false, "why": "That is the opioids row."}], "teach": "Her key lists hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status and respiratory depression for beta-blocker overdose. Calcium channel blocker overdose shares the hypotension and bradycardia, but the patient is often alert.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-016", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-antidotes", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "multi": true, "stem": "Which of the following are listed as antidotes for beta-blocker overdose? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Glucagon", "correct": true, "why": "Listed: glucagon increases cardiac cellular cAMP to increase heart rate."}, {"t": "Vasopressors", "correct": true, "why": "Listed: vasopressors increase blood pressure."}, {"t": "Intravenous 20% fatty acid emulsion", "correct": true, "why": "Listed as IV FE: a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough."}, {"t": "Octreotide", "correct": false, "why": "Octreotide is the sulfonylurea and meglitinide antidote."}, {"t": "Naloxone", "correct": false, "why": "Naloxone is the opioid antidote."}], "teach": "Beta-blocker overdose leaves a slow heart and low blood pressure, and the six antidotes attack that from different sides. Atropine raises heart rate; glucagon raises it through cardiac cAMP; calcium is driven into cardiac cells; vasopressors raise blood pressure; IV 20% fatty acid emulsion acts as a sink for the drug if it is lipophilic enough; HIET has inotropic effects by mechanisms not understood. The calcium channel blocker row says same as above, so the same six apply.", "note": "The key writes the abbreviation HIET without expanding it. The antidote column writes IV FE; the mechanism column writes \"i.v. 20% fatty acid emulsion\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-017", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-atropine", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "stem": "In beta-blocker overdose, what is the mechanism of action listed for atropine?", "options": [{"t": "Increase heart rate", "correct": true, "why": "Beta-blocker overdose causes bradycardia; atropine is listed to increase heart rate, countering it."}, {"t": "Drive calcium into cardiac cells", "correct": false, "why": "That is calcium."}, {"t": "Act as a sink for a lipophilic drug", "correct": false, "why": "That is the IV 20% fatty acid emulsion."}, {"t": "Increase blood pressure", "correct": false, "why": "That is the vasopressors."}], "teach": "Each of the six beta-blocker antidotes has its own entry in the mechanism column. Atropine increases heart rate, against the bradycardia of the overdose. In the organophosphate row, atropine is listed instead as a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-018", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-calcium", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "stem": "In beta-blocker or calcium channel blocker overdose, what is calcium given to do?", "options": [{"t": "Try to drive calcium into cardiac cells", "correct": true, "why": "A calcium channel blocker, as the name says, blocks calcium channels; giving calcium tries to drive more calcium into cardiac cells. The beta-blocker row lists the same step, and the calcium channel blocker row repeats it."}, {"t": "Increase cardiac cellular cAMP", "correct": false, "why": "That is glucagon."}, {"t": "Act as a sink for a lipophilic drug", "correct": false, "why": "That is the IV 20% fatty acid emulsion."}, {"t": "Counteract the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels", "correct": false, "why": "That is sodium bicarbonate in the tricyclic antidepressant row."}], "teach": "Beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdoses both leave the patient hypotensive and bradycardic, and her key lists calcium among the antidotes for both, with the mechanism try to drive calcium into cardiac cells. The other cardiac entries work by other routes: atropine and glucagon raise heart rate, vasopressors raise blood pressure, and the fatty acid emulsion soaks up a lipophilic drug.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Beta blockers’, ‘Calcium channel blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-019", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-glucagon", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "stem": "In beta-blocker overdose, what is the mechanism of action listed for glucagon?", "options": [{"t": "Increase cardiac cAMP to increase heart rate", "correct": true, "why": "Beta-blocker overdose slows the heart; glucagon raises cardiac cellular cAMP, and that increases heart rate."}, {"t": "Inhibit insulin release from the pancreas", "correct": false, "why": "That is octreotide, in the sulfonylurea row."}, {"t": "Try to drive calcium into cardiac cells", "correct": false, "why": "That is calcium."}, {"t": "Counteract the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels", "correct": false, "why": "That is sodium bicarbonate in tricyclic antidepressant overdose."}], "teach": "Beta-blocker overdose causes bradycardia. Glucagon's listed mechanism counters it: glucagon increases cardiac cellular cAMP, which increases heart rate. Atropine reaches the same goal, a faster heart rate, by its own route. In this table glucagon is listed for its cardiac effect, not for blood sugar; the sulfonylurea row uses octreotide.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-020", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-hiet", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "stem": "In beta-blocker overdose, what is known about the mechanism of HIET?", "options": [{"t": "Its inotropic mechanisms are not understood", "correct": true, "why": "HIET is listed for its inotropic effects, but unlike the other five beta-blocker antidotes it has no stated mechanism: the key says how it produces those effects is not understood."}, {"t": "It increases cardiac cellular cyclic AMP (cAMP)", "correct": false, "why": "That is glucagon."}, {"t": "It acts as a sink for a lipophilic drug", "correct": false, "why": "That is the IV 20% fatty acid emulsion."}, {"t": "It increases heart rate", "correct": false, "why": "That is atropine."}], "teach": "HIET is listed among the antidotes for beta-blocker overdose, and by same as above for calcium channel blocker overdose, for its inotropic effects. Unlike the other five, it has no mechanism in the key: the mechanisms of those inotropic effects are not understood. Each of the others has one: atropine and glucagon raise heart rate, calcium is driven into cardiac cells, vasopressors raise blood pressure, and the fatty acid emulsion is a sink for a lipophilic drug.", "note": "HIET is written as an abbreviation in her key and is not expanded there or in the transcript.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-021", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-pressors", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "stem": "In beta-blocker overdose, what is the mechanism of action listed for vasopressors?", "options": [{"t": "Increase blood pressure", "correct": true, "why": "Beta-blocker overdose causes hypotension; vasopressors are listed to increase blood pressure, countering it."}, {"t": "Increase heart rate", "correct": false, "why": "That is atropine (glucagon also raises heart rate, through cAMP)."}, {"t": "Drive calcium into cardiac cells", "correct": false, "why": "That is calcium."}, {"t": "Act as a sink for a lipophilic drug", "correct": false, "why": "That is the IV 20% fatty acid emulsion."}], "teach": "Her key: vasopressors increase blood pressure, against the hypotension of beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose. In the tricyclic antidepressant row, dopamine or norepinephrine serve the same purpose, as supportive care to maintain blood pressure.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-022", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-fe", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers'", "stem": "In beta-blocker overdose, what is the mechanism of intravenous (IV) 20% fatty acid emulsion?", "options": [{"t": "It acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough", "correct": true, "why": "The emulsion can take up the drug only if the drug is lipophilic enough; it then holds the drug like a sink. The condition is part of the mechanism."}, {"t": "It increases cardiac cellular cyclic AMP (cAMP)", "correct": false, "why": "That is glucagon."}, {"t": "It binds the drug in an antibody complex that is renally eliminated", "correct": false, "why": "Binding and renal elimination is the digoxin antibody fragments row."}, {"t": "It increases blood pressure", "correct": false, "why": "That is the vasopressors."}], "teach": "The antidote column lists IV FE; the mechanism column spells it out as i.v. 20% fatty acid emulsion, which acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough. The condition matters: the drug has to be lipophilic enough to move into the emulsion.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-023", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-bb-match", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each beta-blocker overdose antidote with the mechanism listed for it.", "left": ["Atropine", "Calcium", "Glucagon", "HIET", "Vasopressors", "Intravenous (IV) 20% fatty acid emulsion"], "right": ["Increase heart rate", "Try to drive calcium into cardiac cells", "Increase cardiac cellular cyclic AMP (cAMP) to increase heart rate", "Mechanisms of inotropic effects not understood", "Increase blood pressure", "Acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough"], "pairs": [{"l": "Atropine", "r": "Increase heart rate", "why": "Atropine: increase heart rate."}, {"l": "Calcium", "r": "Try to drive calcium into cardiac cells", "why": "Calcium: try to drive calcium into cardiac cells."}, {"l": "Glucagon", "r": "Increase cardiac cellular cyclic AMP (cAMP) to increase heart rate", "why": "Glucagon: increase cardiac cellular cAMP to increase heart rate."}, {"l": "HIET", "r": "Mechanisms of inotropic effects not understood", "why": "HIET: mechanisms of inotropic effects not understood."}, {"l": "Vasopressors", "r": "Increase blood pressure", "why": "Vasopressors: increase blood pressure."}, {"l": "Intravenous (IV) 20% fatty acid emulsion", "r": "Acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough", "why": "i.v. 20% fatty acid emulsion: acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough."}], "teach": "The beta blocker row is the longest in the table: six antidotes, each with its own mechanism. Atropine and glucagon both raise heart rate (glucagon through cardiac cAMP); calcium is driven into cardiac cells; vasopressors raise blood pressure; the fatty acid emulsion is a sink for a lipophilic drug; HIET's inotropic mechanisms are not understood. The calcium channel blocker row repeats the list.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-024", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "tell", "concept": "tbl-bb-vs-ccb", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Beta blockers', special considerations column", "stem": "What can distinguish between beta-blocker overdose and calcium channel blocker overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Beta-blocker overdose may have altered mental status", "correct": true, "why": "Her key: BB OD may have altered mental status; calcium channel blocker overdose is listed as often alert."}, {"t": "Calcium channel blocker overdose may have altered mental status", "correct": false, "why": "Reversed: the calcium channel blocker row says often alert."}, {"t": "Beta-blocker overdose may have bradycardia", "correct": false, "why": "Both rows list bradycardia, so it does not distinguish them."}, {"t": "Calcium channel blocker overdose may have hypotension", "correct": false, "why": "Both rows list hypotension, so it does not distinguish them."}], "teach": "Both overdoses cause hypotension and bradycardia, and the antidotes are the same. Her key's answer to the special-considerations question: the beta-blocker overdose may have altered mental status, while the calcium channel blocker patient is often alert.", "note": "Key abbreviations: BB OD is beta-blocker overdose.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Beta blockers’, ‘Calcium channel blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-025", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-ccb-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Calcium channel blockers'", "stem": "Which entry gives the symptoms of calcium channel blocker overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Hypotension, bradycardia, but often alert", "correct": true, "why": "Calcium channel blocker overdose drops blood pressure and slows the heart as beta-blocker overdose does, but the patient is often alert; that alertness is what separates the two."}, {"t": "Hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status, respiratory depression", "correct": false, "why": "That is beta blockers."}, {"t": "Somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, decreased pupils", "correct": false, "why": "That is opioids."}, {"t": "Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures", "correct": false, "why": "That is tricyclic antidepressants."}], "teach": "Her key: calcium channel blocker overdose causes hypotension and bradycardia, but the patient is often alert. That alertness is the answer to the beta blocker special-considerations question.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Calcium channel blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-026", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-ccb-antidotes", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Calcium channel blockers'", "stem": "What antidotes, with what mechanisms, are listed for calcium channel blocker overdose?", "options": [{"t": "The same as for beta-blocker overdose", "correct": true, "why": "Both overdoses produce hypotension and bradycardia, and the key treats them with the same six antidotes and mechanisms, writing same as above for BB OD in both columns."}, {"t": "The beta-blocker list without glucagon", "correct": false, "why": "Her key says \"same as above for BB OD\", glucagon included."}, {"t": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments", "correct": false, "why": "That is the digoxin antidote."}, {"t": "Intravenous fluids and sodium bicarbonate", "correct": false, "why": "That is the tricyclic antidepressant row."}], "teach": "Beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdoses look alike, with hypotension and bradycardia, and her key treats them alike: the calcium channel blocker row writes same as above for BB OD in both the antidote and mechanism columns. The six are atropine and glucagon to raise heart rate, calcium driven into cardiac cells, vasopressors for blood pressure, IV 20% fatty acid emulsion as a sink for a lipophilic drug, and HIET. Only the symptoms differ: the calcium channel blocker patient is often alert.", "note": "Keyed on her key, which repeats the full beta-blocker list for calcium channel blockers and leaves the special-considerations cell empty.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Calcium channel blockers’, ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-027", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-ccb-antidotes-sata", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Calcium channel blockers'", "multi": true, "stem": "Which of the following are listed as antidotes for calcium channel blocker overdose? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Atropine", "correct": true, "why": "Increases heart rate against the bradycardia; the calcium channel blocker row repeats the beta-blocker list."}, {"t": "Calcium", "correct": true, "why": "Tries to drive calcium into cardiac cells; the calcium channel blocker row repeats the beta-blocker list."}, {"t": "HIET", "correct": true, "why": "Listed for its inotropic effects, by mechanisms not understood; the calcium channel blocker row repeats the beta-blocker list."}, {"t": "Pralidoxime", "correct": false, "why": "Pralidoxime is paired with atropine for organophosphate insecticides; it removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase."}, {"t": "Flumazenil", "correct": false, "why": "Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote."}], "teach": "Her key's calcium channel blocker antidotes are \"same as above for BB OD\": atropine, calcium, glucagon, HIET, vasopressors and IV 20% fatty acid emulsion. The rows differ in symptoms (often alert), not in antidotes.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Calcium channel blockers’, ‘Beta blockers’", "dupOf": "TBL3-026"},
{"id": "TBL3-028", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-dig-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Digoxin'", "stem": "Which entry gives the symptoms and diagnostic method for digoxin overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Bradycardia, arrhythmias, high serum potassium; blood levels can be measured", "correct": true, "why": "Digoxin overdose disturbs the heart's rate and rhythm (bradycardia, arrhythmias) and, alone in the table, raises serum potassium, with nausea/vomiting. Digoxin levels can be measured in the blood, though after Fab is given they no longer guide dosing."}, {"t": "Nausea/vomiting and signs of liver failure; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "That is the acetaminophen row."}, {"t": "CNS depression and relatively normal vitals; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "That is the benzodiazepines row."}, {"t": "Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation; blood levels can be measured", "correct": false, "why": "Those are tricyclic antidepressant symptoms; the key lists no blood-level diagnostic method for that row."}], "teach": "Her key for digoxin: bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium and nausea/vomiting, and digoxin levels can be measured in the blood. Elevated serum potassium is the finding no other row lists.", "note": "The key spells arrhythmias as \"arrythmias\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Digoxin’"},
{"id": "TBL3-029", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-dig-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Digoxin'", "stem": "What is the antidote for digoxin overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments", "correct": true, "why": "The fragments bind digoxin in the blood so it can no longer act, and the bound drug is renally eliminated: inactivation by an antibody."}, {"t": "Intravenous 20% fatty acid emulsion", "correct": false, "why": "That is on the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker list: a sink for a lipophilic drug."}, {"t": "Deferoxamine", "correct": false, "why": "That is the iron antidote."}, {"t": "Atropine and pralidoxime", "correct": false, "why": "Those are the organophosphate antidotes."}], "teach": "Her key names digoxin specific antibody fragments (Fab) as the digoxin antidote. On 10/19 she called digoxin immune Fab an antibody made to grab digoxin and clear it from the blood. It is the example of inactivation by an antibody.", "quote": "One form of antibody made to grab digoxin that somebody's overdosed on, it grabs it and nicely clears it out of the blood.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Digoxin’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-030", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-dig-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Digoxin'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of digoxin specific antibody fragments?", "options": [{"t": "Binds digoxin, which will be renally eliminated", "correct": true, "why": "The fragments are an inactivator: they bind digoxin so it cannot interact with its target, and the digoxin-Fab complex is renally eliminated."}, {"t": "Chelates the circulating ferric iron", "correct": false, "why": "That is deferoxamine."}, {"t": "Increases cardiac cellular cAMP", "correct": false, "why": "That is glucagon."}, {"t": "Renal ion trapping to enhance elimination", "correct": false, "why": "That is sodium bicarbonate for salicylates; the antibody complex is renally eliminated, but by binding, not ion trapping."}], "teach": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments work by inactivation, not at a receptor. An inactivator binds the toxin so it cannot reach its target, and the toxin-inactivator complex is then cleared; here the fragments bind digoxin and the complex is renally eliminated. Because the bound digoxin is still in the blood, total digoxin rises after Fab is given, which is why serum levels stop guiding further Fab doses.", "quote": "One form of antibody made to grab digoxin that somebody's overdosed on, it grabs it and nicely clears it out of the blood.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Digoxin’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-038"},
{"id": "TBL3-031", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cardio", "skill": "apply", "concept": "tbl-dig-levels", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Digoxin', special considerations column", "stem": "Once you give the antidote for digoxin overdose, digoxin specific antibody fragments (Fab), why is checking serum digoxin concentrations not helpful?", "options": [{"t": "The measured total, free plus Fab-bound digoxin, is increased", "correct": true, "why": "Her key: the total dig concentration (free and bound to Fab) will be increased; you cannot use this measurement to calculate more Fab doses."}, {"t": "The Fab fragments clear digoxin in minutes, so the level reads zero", "correct": false, "why": "The key says the total concentration increases, not that it falls to zero."}, {"t": "The Fab fragments block the assay from detecting digoxin", "correct": false, "why": "The key says the measured total, free plus bound, goes up."}, {"t": "The level falls as the complex is renally eliminated", "correct": false, "why": "The digoxin-Fab complex is renally eliminated, but the problem her key names is the opposite: the measured total (free plus bound to Fab) goes up, so it cannot be used to calculate more Fab doses."}], "teach": "Fab binds digoxin in the blood. After it is given, the total digoxin concentration, free plus bound to Fab, is increased, so the measured level no longer tells how much more Fab is needed. That is her key's answer to the special-considerations question.", "note": "Key abbreviations: \"dig\" is digoxin; Fab is the antibody fragments.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Digoxin’"},
{"id": "TBL3-032", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-tca-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Tricyclic Antidepressants'", "multi": true, "stem": "Which of the following are listed as symptoms of tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Dilated pupils", "correct": true, "why": "TCA overdose dilates the pupils, the opposite of the decreased pupils of opioid overdose."}, {"t": "Dry mouth", "correct": true, "why": "Dry mouth is in the TCA row; it is the opposite of the salivation in the organophosphate SLUDGE picture."}, {"t": "Elongated QRS interval", "correct": true, "why": "The key pairs this finding with a treatment: sodium bicarbonate is given for the elongated QRS interval because it counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels."}, {"t": "Bradycardia", "correct": false, "why": "Bradycardia is listed for beta blockers, calcium channel blockers and digoxin; the TCA row lists tachycardia."}, {"t": "Decreased pupils", "correct": false, "why": "That is the opioid row; TCAs dilate the pupils."}], "teach": "Her key's TCA picture has two parts. First, a fast heart, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation and seizures. Second, cardiac effects: an elongated QRS interval and reduced contractility, sometimes with vasodilation. The treatments match the cardiac part: sodium bicarbonate is given for the elongated QRS interval because it counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels, and intravenous fluids with dopamine or norepinephrine are supportive care to maintain blood pressure.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’; transcript 10/14"},
{"id": "TBL3-033", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-tca-antidotes", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Tricyclic Antidepressants'", "multi": true, "stem": "Which of the following are listed as treatments for tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Intravenous fluids", "correct": true, "why": "Listed: supportive care to maintain blood pressure."}, {"t": "Dopamine or norepinephrine", "correct": true, "why": "Listed: supportive care to maintain blood pressure."}, {"t": "Sodium bicarbonate for an elongated QRS interval", "correct": true, "why": "Listed: it counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels."}, {"t": "Flumazenil", "correct": false, "why": "Not in the TCA row; flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote."}, {"t": "Glucagon", "correct": false, "why": "Glucagon is on the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker list, to increase heart rate; the TCA row lists tachycardia and is treated with fluids, dopamine or norepinephrine, and sodium bicarbonate."}], "teach": "Her key lists three treatments for TCA overdose: intravenous fluids, dopamine or norepinephrine, and sodium bicarbonate for an elongated QRS interval. The first two are supportive care to maintain blood pressure; the bicarbonate acts on the sodium channel problem.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’"},
{"id": "TBL3-034", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-tca-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Tricyclic Antidepressants'", "stem": "In tricyclic antidepressant overdose, what do intravenous fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine do, and what does sodium bicarbonate do?", "options": [{"t": "Fluids and pressors: support blood pressure; bicarbonate: counteracts sodium-channel inhibition", "correct": true, "why": "Her key: mostly supportive care, trying to maintain blood pressure; sodium bicarbonate counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels."}, {"t": "Fluids and pressors: support blood pressure; bicarbonate: renal ion trapping", "correct": false, "why": "Renal ion trapping is the bicarbonate mechanism in the salicylates row, not the TCA row."}, {"t": "Fluids and pressors: increase drug elimination; bicarbonate: chelates the drug", "correct": false, "why": "Fluids and pressors are supportive care to maintain blood pressure, not a way to eliminate the drug, and chelation is for metals such as iron. Bicarbonate's TCA role is counteracting the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels."}, {"t": "Fluids and pressors: antagonize the drug at its receptor; bicarbonate: replenishes glutathione", "correct": false, "why": "Fluids and pressors do not act at a receptor; they support blood pressure. Replenishing glutathione is N-acetylcysteine's action for acetaminophen; bicarbonate's TCA role is counteracting sodium-channel inhibition."}], "teach": "TCA overdose causes cardiac trouble on two fronts, and the key matches a treatment to each. Reduced contractility and sometimes vasodilation bear on blood pressure, so intravenous fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine are given as supportive care to maintain it. The elongated QRS interval is treated with sodium bicarbonate, which counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels. The same bicarbonate does something different for salicylates: renal ion trapping.", "note": "The row cites Basic and Clinical Pharmacology, Chapt 58, and has no special-considerations question.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’"},
{"id": "TBL3-035", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "tell", "concept": "tbl-bicarb-two-rows", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, rows 'Tricyclic Antidepressants' and 'Salicylates'", "stem": "Sodium bicarbonate is used for two poisonings. Which pairing of poisoning and mechanism is correct?", "options": [{"t": "Tricyclic antidepressants: counteracts sodium-channel inhibition; salicylates: renal ion trapping", "correct": true, "why": "Her key: in the TCA row, sodium bicarbonate counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels; in the salicylates row, renal ion trapping to enhance elimination."}, {"t": "Tricyclic antidepressants: renal ion trapping; salicylates: counteracts sodium-channel inhibition", "correct": false, "why": "The mechanisms are swapped. Ion trapping works on a weak acid like salicylate, which bicarbonate charges in basic urine; in TCA overdose bicarbonate acts on the heart, counteracting the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels."}, {"t": "Both: renal ion trapping to enhance elimination", "correct": false, "why": "Ion trapping needs a weakly acidic poison that bicarbonate can charge in the urine, which is salicylate. For TCAs the key gives bicarbonate a cardiac role, against sodium-channel inhibition and the elongated QRS interval."}, {"t": "Both: counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels", "correct": false, "why": "The sodium-channel mechanism is the TCA entry. For salicylates bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, so the charged weak acid is not reabsorbed and is excreted."}], "teach": "The same drug carries two mechanisms in the table. For salicylates, IV sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic so the weak acid is trapped and eliminated (renal ion trapping). For tricyclic antidepressants, sodium bicarbonate is given for an elongated QRS interval and counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels.", "quote": "if we have a person that's overdosed on salicylates on some of our barbiturates, they are mildly acidic. ... We'll hang a bag of sodium bicarb on a patient, that'll make their urine a bit more basic", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’, ‘Salicylates’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-036", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-opioid-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Opioids'", "stem": "Which entry gives the symptoms of opioid overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, decreased pupils", "correct": true, "why": "Opioid overdose depresses consciousness (somnolence, coma) and also slows heart and breathing and shrinks the pupils. The falling vital signs separate it from benzodiazepine overdose, where vitals stay relatively normal."}, {"t": "Somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, dilated pupils", "correct": false, "why": "The pupils are decreased in opioid overdose; dilated pupils are the tricyclic antidepressant row."}, {"t": "Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis", "correct": false, "why": "That is salicylates."}, {"t": "CNS depression and relatively normal vitals", "correct": false, "why": "That is benzodiazepines."}], "teach": "Her key: somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, and decreased pupils. The falling heart and breathing rates separate opioids from benzodiazepines, whose vitals are relatively normal.", "note": "The key writes \"decrease pupils\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Opioids’"},
{"id": "TBL3-037", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-opioid-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Opioids'", "stem": "What is the antidote for opioid overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Naloxone", "correct": true, "why": "The overdosed opioid acts as an agonist at the opioid receptor; naloxone is an antagonist of that receptor and blocks the effect."}, {"t": "Flumazenil", "correct": false, "why": "That is the benzodiazepine antidote."}, {"t": "Octreotide", "correct": false, "why": "That is the sulfonylurea and meglitinide antidote."}, {"t": "Atropine", "correct": false, "why": "Atropine is in the beta blocker and organophosphate rows."}], "teach": "When the poison acts as an agonist, the antidote can be a receptor antagonist. Naloxone is the first example: an antagonist of the opioid receptor, so it blocks the effect of the overdosed opioid. Her key calls it a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors and warns that in chronic opioid users it can trigger withdrawal.", "quote": "Naloxone is basically an antagonist of an opioid receptor", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Opioids’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-038", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-opioid-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Opioids'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of naloxone?", "options": [{"t": "Competitive antagonist of opioid receptors", "correct": true, "why": "The overdosed opioid is agonizing the opioid receptor; naloxone competes with it for the same receptor and blocks it."}, {"t": "Competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor", "correct": false, "why": "That is flumazenil."}, {"t": "Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist", "correct": false, "why": "That is atropine in the organophosphate row."}, {"t": "Binds the opioid, which is then renally eliminated", "correct": false, "why": "Binding and renal elimination is the digoxin antibody fragments row."}], "teach": "Her key: naloxone is a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors. The overdosed opioid is the agonist; naloxone competes with it at the receptor. Flumazenil is the same kind of treatment at the benzodiazepine receptor.", "quote": "Naloxone is basically an antagonist of an opioid receptor", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Opioids’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-040"},
{"id": "TBL3-039", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "apply", "concept": "tbl-opioid-withdrawal", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Opioids', special considerations column", "stem": "What is a concern when using naloxone in chronic opioid users?", "options": [{"t": "It can trigger withdrawal", "correct": true, "why": "Naloxone competitively blocks opioid receptors. In a chronic opioid user, blocking the opioid's action can trigger withdrawal."}, {"t": "It loses effectiveness about 8 hours after overdose", "correct": false, "why": "That is the acetaminophen antidote."}, {"t": "It raises the total measured drug level", "correct": false, "why": "That is the digoxin antibody fragments special consideration."}, {"t": "It has more adverse effects than the alternative", "correct": false, "why": "That describes ethanol compared with fomepizole."}], "teach": "Naloxone works by competing with the opioid at its receptor and blocking it. That reverses an overdose, but in a chronic opioid user, cutting off the opioid's action can trigger withdrawal. Flumazenil carries the parallel concern for the same reason: it blocks the benzodiazepine receptor and can cause withdrawal symptoms in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Opioids’"},
{"id": "TBL3-040", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "cns", "skill": "tell", "concept": "tbl-withdrawal-pair", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, special considerations for flumazenil and naloxone", "stem": "Which two antidotes carry the concern that they can cause withdrawal in a dependent patient?", "options": [{"t": "Flumazenil and naloxone", "correct": true, "why": "Flumazenil: withdrawal symptoms in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient. Naloxone: can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users."}, {"t": "Naloxone and N-acetylcysteine", "correct": false, "why": "The N-acetylcysteine concern is its time point (about 8 hours), not withdrawal."}, {"t": "Flumazenil and fomepizole", "correct": false, "why": "The fomepizole entry is its advantage over ethanol, not withdrawal."}, {"t": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments and naloxone", "correct": false, "why": "The antibody fragments concern is the misleading serum level, not withdrawal."}], "teach": "Two of the receptor antagonists in the table carry the same concern. Flumazenil can cause withdrawal symptoms in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient, and naloxone can trigger withdrawal in chronic opioid users. Both compete with the overdosed agonist at its receptor.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Benzodiazepines’, ‘Opioids’"},
{"id": "TBL3-041", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-sal-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Salicylates'", "stem": "In salicylate poisoning, which sequence of findings is listed?", "options": [{"t": "Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis", "correct": true, "why": "The key gives the order: hyperventilation brings respiratory alkalosis first, then metabolic acidosis follows. Salicylate is itself mildly acidic, which is also why bicarbonate can trap it in basic urine."}, {"t": "Metabolic acidosis first, then hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis", "correct": false, "why": "Reversed: the respiratory alkalosis comes first."}, {"t": "Anion-gap metabolic acidosis with ocular toxicity", "correct": false, "why": "That is methanol."}, {"t": "Respiratory depression and altered mental status", "correct": false, "why": "Those are in the beta-blocker row."}], "teach": "The salicylate row runs in order: hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis first, then metabolic acidosis, along with hyperthermia, confusion and seizures. Salicylate is mildly acidic, and that chemistry is the basis of its treatment: IV sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, the salicylate becomes charged, cannot be reabsorbed, and is excreted (renal ion trapping).", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Salicylates’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-042", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-sal-other", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Salicylates'", "stem": "Besides the acid-base changes, which findings are listed for salicylate poisoning?", "options": [{"t": "Hyperthermia, confusion and seizures", "correct": true, "why": "Alongside the acid-base sequence (respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis), the salicylate row adds hyperthermia, confusion and seizures."}, {"t": "Ocular toxicity and blindness", "correct": false, "why": "That is methanol."}, {"t": "Elevated serum potassium and arrhythmias", "correct": false, "why": "That is digoxin."}, {"t": "Somnolence, coma and decreased pupils", "correct": false, "why": "That is opioids."}], "teach": "Salicylate poisoning has an acid-base sequence, hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis and then metabolic acidosis, plus hyperthermia, confusion and seizures. The treatment uses the drug's chemistry: salicylate is mildly acidic, so IV sodium bicarbonate, by making the urine more basic, charges it and traps it in the urine for elimination.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Salicylates’"},
{"id": "TBL3-043", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-sal-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Salicylates'", "stem": "What treatment is listed for salicylate poisoning?", "options": [{"t": "Supportive care, IV sodium bicarbonate", "correct": true, "why": "Salicylate is mildly acidic; IV sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, so the salicylate becomes charged, is not reabsorbed, and is excreted. Supportive care covers the rest."}, {"t": "Ethanol or fomepizole", "correct": false, "why": "That is the toxic alcohols row."}, {"t": "IV fluids, dopamine or norepinephrine", "correct": false, "why": "That is the tricyclic antidepressant row, to maintain blood pressure."}, {"t": "Octreotide", "correct": false, "why": "That is the sulfonylurea and meglitinide antidote."}], "teach": "Salicylate poisoning is treated with supportive care and intravenous sodium bicarbonate. On 10/19 she used salicylates as the example of ion trapping: a bag of sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic.", "quote": "if we have a person that's overdosed on salicylates on some of our barbiturates, they are mildly acidic. ... We'll hang a bag of sodium bicarb on a patient, that'll make their urine a bit more basic", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Salicylates’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-044", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-sal-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Salicylates'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of intravenous (IV) sodium bicarbonate in salicylate poisoning?", "options": [{"t": "Renal ion trapping to enhance elimination", "correct": true, "why": "Bicarbonate raises urine pH, the mildly acidic salicylate takes a negative charge, and the reabsorption transporters, which mostly move uncharged molecules, leave it in the urine to be excreted."}, {"t": "Inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase", "correct": false, "why": "That is ethanol or fomepizole."}, {"t": "Chelates the salicylate in the plasma", "correct": false, "why": "Chelation is for metals such as iron."}, {"t": "Replenishes glutathione", "correct": false, "why": "That is N-acetylcysteine."}], "teach": "Her key: renal ion trapping to enhance elimination. Salicylate is mildly acidic; bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, the salicylate carries a negative charge there, and the charged molecule is not reabsorbed. That is the toxicokinetic treatment of enhancing renal elimination.", "quote": "if we have a person that's overdosed on salicylates on some of our barbiturates, they are mildly acidic. ... We'll hang a bag of sodium bicarb on a patient, that'll make their urine a bit more basic", "note": "The row cites Basic and Clinical Pharmacology, Chapt 58, and has no special-considerations question.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Salicylates’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-024"},
{"id": "TBL3-045", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-su-symptom", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Sulfonylureas and miglitinide'", "stem": "Which symptom is listed for sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Hypoglycemia", "correct": true, "why": "Hypoglycemia is the single symptom in this row. The antidote, octreotide, inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, which is how it counters the low blood sugar."}, {"t": "Hyperthermia", "correct": false, "why": "That is salicylates."}, {"t": "Elevated serum potassium", "correct": false, "why": "That is digoxin."}, {"t": "Ocular toxicity", "correct": false, "why": "That is methanol."}], "teach": "The sulfonylurea and meglitinide row has a single symptom: hypoglycemia. The antidote, octreotide, works on the insulin release that causes it.", "note": "The key spells meglitinide as \"miglitinide\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Sulfonylureas and miglitinide’"},
{"id": "TBL3-046", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-su-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Sulfonylureas and miglitinide'", "stem": "What is the antidote for sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Octreotide", "correct": true, "why": "Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas; holding back insulin release counters the hypoglycemia of the overdose."}, {"t": "Glucagon", "correct": false, "why": "Glucagon is listed in the beta blocker row, for its cardiac cAMP effect."}, {"t": "Naloxone", "correct": false, "why": "That is the opioid antidote."}, {"t": "Flumazenil", "correct": false, "why": "That is the benzodiazepine antidote."}], "teach": "Sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose causes hypoglycemia. Octreotide is listed because it inhibits insulin release from the pancreas; holding back insulin release treats the low blood sugar. Glucagon also appears in the table, but in the beta-blocker row, for its cardiac cAMP effect on heart rate.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Sulfonylureas and miglitinide’"},
{"id": "TBL3-047", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "metabolic", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-su-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Sulfonylureas and miglitinide'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of octreotide in sulfonylurea overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Inhibits insulin release from the pancreas", "correct": true, "why": "The overdose causes hypoglycemia; octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, and holding back insulin release counters the low blood sugar."}, {"t": "Increases cardiac cellular cAMP", "correct": false, "why": "That is glucagon in beta-blocker overdose."}, {"t": "Replenishes glutathione", "correct": false, "why": "That is N-acetylcysteine."}, {"t": "Competitive antagonist of opioid receptors", "correct": false, "why": "That is naloxone."}], "teach": "Her key: octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas. The overdose causes hypoglycemia; holding back insulin release treats it. The row has no special-considerations question.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Sulfonylureas and miglitinide’"},
{"id": "TBL3-048", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-iron-timeline", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each time after an iron overdose with the findings listed for it.", "left": ["Within 1 to 6 hours", "Within 6 to 24 hours", "In 2 to 5 days"], "right": ["Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea", "Lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting, bloody diarrhea, shock, hypotension and tachycardia", "Liver dysfunction and failure possible"], "pairs": [{"l": "Within 1 to 6 hours", "r": "Vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea", "why": "The first bullet of the iron row."}, {"l": "Within 6 to 24 hours", "r": "Lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting, bloody diarrhea, shock, hypotension and tachycardia", "why": "The second and third bullets, both within 6 to 24 hours."}, {"l": "In 2 to 5 days", "r": "Liver dysfunction and failure possible", "why": "The last bullet of the iron row."}], "teach": "Her key gives iron a timeline. Within 1 to 6 hours: vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea. Within 6 to 24 hours: lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting, bloody diarrhea and shock, with hypotension and tachycardia. Liver dysfunction and failure are possible in 2 to 5 days.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Iron’"},
{"id": "TBL3-049", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-iron-early", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Iron'", "stem": "Within what time after an iron overdose do vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea appear?", "options": [{"t": "1 to 6 hours", "correct": true, "why": "The gut symptoms come first: vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea within 1 to 6 hours. The systemic signs (lethargy, coma, shock) follow at 6 to 24 hours, and the liver in 2 to 5 days."}, {"t": "6 to 24 hours", "correct": false, "why": "That window is for lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting, bloody diarrhea, shock, hypotension and tachycardia."}, {"t": "2 to 5 days", "correct": false, "why": "That is when liver dysfunction and failure are possible."}, {"t": "About 8 hours", "correct": false, "why": "About 8 hours is the acetaminophen antidote's time point."}], "teach": "The iron row gives three windows: 1 to 6 hours for vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea; 6 to 24 hours for lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting and diarrhea, shock, hypotension and tachycardia; 2 to 5 days for possible liver dysfunction and failure.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Iron’"},
{"id": "TBL3-050", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-iron-late", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Iron'", "stem": "In iron overdose, when are liver dysfunction and failure possible?", "options": [{"t": "In 2 to 5 days", "correct": true, "why": "Liver involvement is the last stage of the iron timeline: after the gut symptoms (1 to 6 hours) and shock (6 to 24 hours), liver dysfunction and failure are possible in 2 to 5 days."}, {"t": "Within 1 to 6 hours", "correct": false, "why": "That is the window for vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea."}, {"t": "Within 6 to 24 hours", "correct": false, "why": "That is the window for lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting and diarrhea, shock, hypotension and tachycardia."}, {"t": "After 2 weeks", "correct": false, "why": "The iron timeline ends sooner: liver dysfunction and failure are possible within 2 to 5 days, not weeks later."}], "teach": "Liver involvement is the last step of the iron timeline: liver dysfunction and failure are possible in 2 to 5 days. The gastrointestinal symptoms come first, within 1 to 6 hours.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Iron’"},
{"id": "TBL3-051", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-iron-antidote", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Iron'", "stem": "What is the antidote for iron overdose?", "options": [{"t": "Deferoxamine", "correct": true, "why": "Deferoxamine is a heavy metal chelator: it binds circulating ferric iron so the iron cannot act, and the complex is cleared."}, {"t": "Dimercaprol", "correct": false, "why": "On the 10/19 chelator table, dimercaprol chelates gold, arsenic, lead and mercury."}, {"t": "Penicillamine", "correct": false, "why": "On the 10/19 chelator table, penicillamine chelates copper."}, {"t": "Pralidoxime", "correct": false, "why": "That is an organophosphate antidote."}], "teach": "Her key names deferoxamine as the iron antidote. On 10/19 she said two iron chelators have been on the market, deferoxamine and the oral deferasirox; the table lists deferoxamine.", "quote": "We have had two iron chelating agents on the market now, differoxyzine and differracerro.", "note": "The transcript renders deferoxamine and deferasirox as \"differoxyzine\" and \"differracerro\". The iron row cites Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 13th Ed. Chapt e6, while on 10/19 she pointed to DiPiro chapter e9 for the table.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Iron’; transcript 10/19; Treating Poisonings Students Fall 2026 WCAG2,1A_pptx.pdf slide ‘Example Chelating Agents’"},
{"id": "TBL3-052", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-iron-moa", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Iron'", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of deferoxamine?", "options": [{"t": "Chelates the circulating ferric iron", "correct": true, "why": "A chelator is a small molecule that binds a metal more tightly than the body's own macromolecules do; deferoxamine binds circulating ferric iron, inactivating it, and the complex is cleared."}, {"t": "Removes the iron from its target enzyme", "correct": false, "why": "Removing a poison from an enzyme is the pralidoxime entry, for organophosphates."}, {"t": "Renal ion trapping to enhance elimination", "correct": false, "why": "That is sodium bicarbonate for salicylates."}, {"t": "Inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase", "correct": false, "why": "That is ethanol or fomepizole."}], "teach": "Her key: deferoxamine chelates the circulating ferric iron. It is the iron entry of the inactivation approach, with heavy metal chelators binding the metal so it cannot act. The row has no special-considerations question.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Iron’"},
{"id": "TBL3-053", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-op-symptoms", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Organophosphate Insecticides'", "stem": "Which entry gives the symptoms of organophosphate insecticide poisoning?", "options": [{"t": "Acetylcholine excess: SLUDGE or DUMBELLS", "correct": true, "why": "Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up in the synaptic cleft; SLUDGE and DUMBELLS name the signs of that acetylcholine excess."}, {"t": "Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation", "correct": false, "why": "That is the tricyclic antidepressant row."}, {"t": "Hypotension, bradycardia, but often alert", "correct": false, "why": "That is calcium channel blockers."}, {"t": "Somnolence, coma and decreased pupils", "correct": false, "why": "That is the opioids row."}], "teach": "Her key: organophosphate insecticides cause acetylcholine excess, recalled as SLUDGE or DUMBELLS. On 10/19 she explained that organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up in the synaptic cleft.", "quote": "if you are exposed to organophosphate pesticides, you end up with too much acetylcholine in your synaptic cleft. And we'll use atropine to antagonize that.", "note": "The key gives the mnemonics SLUDGE and DUMBELLS without spelling out the letters.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-054", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-op-antidotes", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Organophosphate Insecticides'; her Quiz 3 item 4", "multi": true, "stem": "Which of the following are listed as antidotes for organophosphate insecticide poisoning? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Atropine", "correct": true, "why": "Listed: a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist."}, {"t": "Pralidoxime", "correct": true, "why": "Listed: removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase."}, {"t": "Physostigmine", "correct": false, "why": "Physostigmine was her 10/19 example of a treatment that is a receptor agonist, for belladonna alkaloids; it is not in the table."}, {"t": "Flumazenil", "correct": false, "why": "That is the benzodiazepine antidote."}], "teach": "Her key lists two organophosphate antidotes: atropine and pralidoxime. Atropine blocks the effects of the excess acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors; pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase.", "quote": "One of the things that we can do is to pull off the organophosphate by using pralodoxine. ... it's gonna pull off the organophosphate and put it onto itself. And when it does that, it can be eliminated.", "note": "The transcript renders pralidoxime as \"pralodoxine\". The row cites Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 13th Ed. Chapt e6.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’; transcript 10/19"},
{"id": "TBL3-055", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-op-atropine", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Organophosphate Insecticides'", "stem": "In organophosphate poisoning, what is the mechanism of action listed for atropine?", "options": [{"t": "Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist", "correct": true, "why": "The organophosphate's inhibition of acetylcholinesterase leaves excess acetylcholine; atropine blocks the muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, so that excess cannot act there."}, {"t": "Removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase", "correct": false, "why": "That is pralidoxime."}, {"t": "Inhibits acetylcholinesterase", "correct": false, "why": "That is what the organophosphate does."}, {"t": "Competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor", "correct": false, "why": "That is flumazenil."}], "teach": "Her key: atropine is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist. The organophosphate has inhibited acetylcholinesterase, acetylcholine builds up, and atropine antagonizes it at the receptor. On 10/19 this was her example of a treatment that is a receptor antagonist.", "quote": "if you are exposed to organophosphate pesticides, you end up with too much acetylcholine in your synaptic cleft. And we'll use atropine to antagonize that.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-042"},
{"id": "TBL3-056", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-op-pralidoxime", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, row 'Organophosphate Insecticides'; her Quiz 3 item 4", "stem": "What is the mechanism of action of pralidoxime?", "options": [{"t": "Removes organophosphate (OP) from acetylcholinesterase", "correct": true, "why": "The organophosphate binds acetylcholinesterase and inactivates it; pralidoxime pulls the organophosphate off the enzyme onto itself, restoring the active site, unless the organophosphate has aged."}, {"t": "Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist", "correct": false, "why": "That is atropine."}, {"t": "Breaks down the excess acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft", "correct": false, "why": "Acetylcholinesterase does that once it is freed."}, {"t": "Inhibits acetylcholinesterase to increase acetylcholine", "correct": false, "why": "That is physostigmine, the treatment for anticholinergic (belladonna alkaloid) exposure; it acts like a receptor agonist."}], "teach": "Her key: pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase. On 10/19 she showed it pulling the organophosphate off the enzyme onto itself, after which the complex can be eliminated. It is the example of removing the toxin from its active site.", "quote": "One of the things that we can do is to pull off the organophosphate by using pralodoxine. ... it's gonna pull off the organophosphate and put it onto itself. And when it does that, it can be eliminated.", "note": "The transcript renders pralidoxime as \"pralodoxine\".", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf row ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’; transcript 10/19", "dupOf": "L16-046"},
{"id": "TBL3-057", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "ironop", "skill": "tell", "concept": "tbl-atropine-two-rows", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, rows 'Beta blockers' and 'Organophosphate Insecticides'", "stem": "Atropine is listed for two poisonings. Which pairing of poisoning and mechanism is correct?", "options": [{"t": "Beta blockers: increase heart rate; organophosphates: muscarinic receptor antagonist", "correct": true, "why": "Her key: in the beta blocker row, atropine: increase heart rate; in the organophosphate row, muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist."}, {"t": "Beta blockers: muscarinic receptor antagonist; organophosphates: increase heart rate", "correct": false, "why": "Swapped. In the beta-blocker row atropine is listed by its effect, increasing a heart rate the overdose has slowed; in the organophosphate row it is listed by its action, blocking muscarinic receptors against the acetylcholine excess."}, {"t": "Both: removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase", "correct": false, "why": "That is pralidoxime."}, {"t": "Both: increase cardiac cellular cyclic AMP (cAMP)", "correct": false, "why": "That is glucagon."}], "teach": "The key states atropine's mechanism differently in the two rows. For beta-blocker overdose, it is listed by its effect, increasing heart rate. For organophosphates, it is listed by its action, a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist against the acetylcholine excess.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Beta blockers’, ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’"},
{"id": "TBL3-058", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-blood-levels", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, symptoms/diagnostic methods column", "multi": true, "stem": "For which poisonings is measuring blood levels listed as a diagnostic method? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Acetaminophen", "correct": true, "why": "Acetaminophen levels can be measured in the blood."}, {"t": "Benzodiazepines", "correct": true, "why": "Benzodiazepine levels can be measured in the blood."}, {"t": "Digoxin", "correct": true, "why": "Digoxin levels can be measured in the blood."}, {"t": "Tricyclic antidepressants", "correct": false, "why": "That row lists symptoms and the elongated QRS interval, no blood level."}, {"t": "Organophosphate insecticides", "correct": false, "why": "That row lists acetylcholine excess (SLUDGE or DUMBELLS), no blood level."}], "teach": "Three rows of the key add a blood measurement to the symptoms: acetaminophen, benzodiazepines and digoxin. For digoxin the special-considerations question adds that the level stops being useful once Fab is given.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Acetaminophen’, ‘Benzodiazepines’, ‘Digoxin’"},
{"id": "TBL3-059", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-match-antidote-1", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, antidote column, all 12 rows", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each poisoning with its antidote.", "left": ["Acetaminophen", "Toxic alcohols", "Benzodiazepines", "Opioids", "Digoxin", "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides"], "right": ["N-acetylcysteine", "Ethanol or fomepizole", "Flumazenil", "Naloxone", "Digoxin specific antibody fragments", "Octreotide"], "pairs": [{"l": "Acetaminophen", "r": "N-acetylcysteine", "why": "Acetaminophen row."}, {"l": "Toxic alcohols", "r": "Ethanol or fomepizole", "why": "Toxic alcohols row."}, {"l": "Benzodiazepines", "r": "Flumazenil", "why": "Benzodiazepines row."}, {"l": "Opioids", "r": "Naloxone", "why": "Opioids row."}, {"l": "Digoxin", "r": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments", "why": "Digoxin row."}, {"l": "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides", "r": "Octreotide", "why": "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides row."}], "teach": "Each antidote fits its poison's problem. NAC replenishes the glutathione that conjugates acetaminophen's toxic metabolite. Ethanol or fomepizole block alcohol dehydrogenase so methanol and ethylene glycol are not turned into toxic acids. Flumazenil and naloxone compete with the overdosed drug at the benzodiazepine and opioid receptors. Digoxin antibody fragments bind digoxin for renal elimination. Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, countering the hypoglycemia.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Acetaminophen’, ‘Toxic alcohols’, ‘Benzodiazepines’, ‘Opioids’, ‘Digoxin’, ‘Sulfonylureas and miglitinide’"},
{"id": "TBL3-060", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-match-antidote-2", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each poisoning with the antidotes or treatments listed for it.", "left": ["Beta blockers or calcium channel blockers", "Tricyclic antidepressants", "Salicylates", "Iron", "Organophosphate insecticides"], "right": ["Atropine, calcium, glucagon, HIET, vasopressors, intravenous fatty acid emulsion (IV FE)", "Intravenous fluids, dopamine or norepinephrine, sodium bicarbonate for elongated QRS interval", "Supportive care, intravenous sodium bicarbonate", "Deferoxamine", "Atropine and pralidoxime"], "pairs": [{"l": "Beta blockers or calcium channel blockers", "r": "Atropine, calcium, glucagon, HIET, vasopressors, intravenous fatty acid emulsion (IV FE)", "why": "The beta blocker row; the calcium channel blocker row says same as above."}, {"l": "Tricyclic antidepressants", "r": "Intravenous fluids, dopamine or norepinephrine, sodium bicarbonate for elongated QRS interval", "why": "Tricyclic antidepressants row."}, {"l": "Salicylates", "r": "Supportive care, intravenous sodium bicarbonate", "why": "Salicylates row."}, {"l": "Iron", "r": "Deferoxamine", "why": "Iron row."}, {"l": "Organophosphate insecticides", "r": "Atropine and pralidoxime", "why": "Organophosphate row."}], "teach": "Each multi-treatment row aims at the problem the overdose creates. Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers slow the heart and drop blood pressure, so their six antidotes mostly raise heart rate or pressure, drive calcium into cardiac cells, or soak up a lipophilic drug. Tricyclic antidepressants get fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine for blood pressure, plus bicarbonate against sodium-channel inhibition. Salicylates get bicarbonate for ion trapping. Iron gets deferoxamine, a chelator. Organophosphates get atropine against the acetylcholine excess and pralidoxime to free acetylcholinesterase.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Beta blockers’, ‘Calcium channel blockers’, ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’, ‘Salicylates’, ‘Iron’, ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’"},
{"id": "TBL3-061", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-match-moa-1", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "core": true, "coreWhy": "her table key, mechanism column", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each antidote with its mechanism of action.", "left": ["N-acetylcysteine", "Ethanol or fomepizole", "Flumazenil", "Naloxone", "Digoxin specific antibody fragments", "Octreotide"], "right": ["Replenishes glutathione", "Inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase", "Competitive antagonist of benzodiazepine receptor", "Competitive antagonist of opioid receptors", "Binds digoxin, will be renally eliminated", "Inhibits insulin release from the pancreas"], "pairs": [{"l": "N-acetylcysteine", "r": "Replenishes glutathione", "why": "Acetaminophen row."}, {"l": "Ethanol or fomepizole", "r": "Inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase", "why": "Toxic alcohols row."}, {"l": "Flumazenil", "r": "Competitive antagonist of benzodiazepine receptor", "why": "Benzodiazepines row."}, {"l": "Naloxone", "r": "Competitive antagonist of opioid receptors", "why": "Opioids row."}, {"l": "Digoxin specific antibody fragments", "r": "Binds digoxin, will be renally eliminated", "why": "Digoxin row."}, {"l": "Octreotide", "r": "Inhibits insulin release from the pancreas", "why": "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides row."}], "teach": "The six mechanisms are different kinds of action. N-acetylcysteine supplies what the body uses to clear the toxic metabolite: it replenishes glutathione. Ethanol and fomepizole inhibit the enzyme that would make toxic metabolites, alcohol dehydrogenase. Flumazenil and naloxone are competitive antagonists that block the receptor the overdosed drug is agonizing. Digoxin antibody fragments bind the drug itself, which is then renally eliminated. Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas, countering the hypoglycemia.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Acetaminophen’, ‘Toxic alcohols’, ‘Benzodiazepines’, ‘Opioids’, ‘Digoxin’, ‘Sulfonylureas and miglitinide’"},
{"id": "TBL3-062", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "drug", "concept": "tbl-match-moa-2", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each antidote, in the row named, with its mechanism of action.", "left": ["Deferoxamine (iron)", "Pralidoxime (organophosphates)", "Atropine (organophosphates)", "Sodium bicarbonate (salicylates)", "Sodium bicarbonate (tricyclic antidepressants)", "Glucagon (beta blockers)"], "right": ["Chelates the circulating ferric iron", "Removes organophosphate (OP) from acetylcholinesterase", "Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist", "Renal ion trapping to enhance elimination", "Counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels", "Increase cardiac cellular cyclic AMP (cAMP) to increase heart rate"], "pairs": [{"l": "Deferoxamine (iron)", "r": "Chelates the circulating ferric iron", "why": "Iron row."}, {"l": "Pralidoxime (organophosphates)", "r": "Removes organophosphate (OP) from acetylcholinesterase", "why": "Organophosphate row."}, {"l": "Atropine (organophosphates)", "r": "Muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist", "why": "Organophosphate row."}, {"l": "Sodium bicarbonate (salicylates)", "r": "Renal ion trapping to enhance elimination", "why": "Salicylates row."}, {"l": "Sodium bicarbonate (tricyclic antidepressants)", "r": "Counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels", "why": "Tricyclic antidepressants row."}, {"l": "Glucagon (beta blockers)", "r": "Increase cardiac cellular cyclic AMP (cAMP) to increase heart rate", "why": "Beta blocker row."}], "teach": "Each mechanism here is tied to its poison. Deferoxamine chelates circulating ferric iron, inactivating the metal. For organophosphates, which inhibit acetylcholinesterase, pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from the enzyme and atropine blocks muscarinic receptors against the acetylcholine excess. Sodium bicarbonate has two roles: for salicylates it makes the urine basic so the weak acid is trapped and eliminated; for TCAs it counteracts the inhibition on sodium cardiac channels. Glucagon raises cardiac cAMP to speed the slowed heart of beta-blocker overdose.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Iron’, ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’, ‘Salicylates’, ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’, ‘Beta blockers’"},
{"id": "TBL3-063", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-match-symptoms-1", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each poisoning with the symptoms listed for it.", "left": ["Acetaminophen", "Benzodiazepines", "Opioids", "Beta blockers", "Calcium channel blockers", "Digoxin"], "right": ["Nausea/vomiting; clinical signs of liver failure", "Central nervous system (CNS) depression and relatively normal vitals", "Somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, decreased pupils", "Hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status, respiratory depression", "Hypotension, bradycardia, but often alert", "Bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, nausea/vomiting"], "pairs": [{"l": "Acetaminophen", "r": "Nausea/vomiting; clinical signs of liver failure", "why": "Acetaminophen row."}, {"l": "Benzodiazepines", "r": "Central nervous system (CNS) depression and relatively normal vitals", "why": "Benzodiazepines row."}, {"l": "Opioids", "r": "Somnolence, coma, decreased heart and breath rates, decreased pupils", "why": "Opioids row."}, {"l": "Beta blockers", "r": "Hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status, respiratory depression", "why": "Beta blocker row."}, {"l": "Calcium channel blockers", "r": "Hypotension, bradycardia, but often alert", "why": "Calcium channel blocker row."}, {"l": "Digoxin", "r": "Bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, nausea/vomiting", "why": "Digoxin row."}], "teach": "Six symptom entries to tell apart. Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers share hypotension and bradycardia; altered mental status and respiratory depression mark the beta blocker, and an often-alert patient marks the calcium channel blocker. Opioids lower heart and breathing rates and shrink the pupils; benzodiazepines depress the central nervous system (CNS) with relatively normal vitals. Digoxin adds elevated serum potassium.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Acetaminophen’, ‘Benzodiazepines’, ‘Opioids’, ‘Beta blockers’, ‘Calcium channel blockers’, ‘Digoxin’"},
{"id": "TBL3-064", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-match-symptoms-2", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each poisoning with the symptoms listed for it.", "left": ["Methanol", "Ethylene glycol", "Tricyclic antidepressants", "Salicylates", "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides", "Organophosphate insecticides"], "right": ["Anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity", "Metabolites crystallize in the microvasculature; renal toxicity common", "Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures, elongated QRS interval", "Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis", "Hypoglycemia", "Acetylcholine excess: SLUDGE or DUMBELLS"], "pairs": [{"l": "Methanol", "r": "Anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity", "why": "Toxic alcohols row, methanol."}, {"l": "Ethylene glycol", "r": "Metabolites crystallize in the microvasculature; renal toxicity common", "why": "Toxic alcohols row, ethylene glycol."}, {"l": "Tricyclic antidepressants", "r": "Tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures, elongated QRS interval", "why": "Tricyclic antidepressants row."}, {"l": "Salicylates", "r": "Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, then metabolic acidosis", "why": "Salicylates row."}, {"l": "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides", "r": "Hypoglycemia", "why": "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides row."}, {"l": "Organophosphate insecticides", "r": "Acetylcholine excess: SLUDGE or DUMBELLS", "why": "Organophosphate row."}], "teach": "The other six symptom entries. Methanol metabolites cause anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity; ethylene glycol metabolites crystallize in small vessels and harm the kidney. Tricyclic antidepressants show tachycardia, dilated pupils and dry mouth with an elongated QRS interval; salicylates move from respiratory alkalosis to metabolic acidosis; sulfonylureas cause hypoglycemia; organophosphates cause acetylcholine excess.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Toxic alcohols’, ‘Tricyclic Antidepressants’, ‘Salicylates’, ‘Sulfonylureas and miglitinide’, ‘Organophosphate Insecticides’"},
{"id": "TBL3-065", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "apply", "concept": "tbl-match-special", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each special-considerations question with the answer in her key.", "left": ["Acetaminophen: when does the antidote lose effectiveness, and should you still give it?", "Toxic alcohols: is one of the two treatments better, and why?", "Benzodiazepines: concerns with the antidote?", "Beta blockers: what distinguishes the overdose from a calcium channel blocker overdose?", "Digoxin: why are serum levels not helpful after the antidote?", "Opioids: concern in chronic opioid users?"], "right": ["About 8 hours after overdose; yes", "Fomepizole: stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, fewer adverse effects", "Withdrawal symptoms in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient", "Altered mental status in beta-blocker overdose", "Total digoxin, free and bound to Fab, is increased", "It can trigger withdrawal"], "pairs": [{"l": "Acetaminophen: when does the antidote lose effectiveness, and should you still give it?", "r": "About 8 hours after overdose; yes", "why": "Acetaminophen row."}, {"l": "Toxic alcohols: is one of the two treatments better, and why?", "r": "Fomepizole: stronger alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor, fewer adverse effects", "why": "Toxic alcohols row."}, {"l": "Benzodiazepines: concerns with the antidote?", "r": "Withdrawal symptoms in a benzodiazepine-dependent patient", "why": "Benzodiazepines row."}, {"l": "Beta blockers: what distinguishes the overdose from a calcium channel blocker overdose?", "r": "Altered mental status in beta-blocker overdose", "why": "Beta blocker row."}, {"l": "Digoxin: why are serum levels not helpful after the antidote?", "r": "Total digoxin, free and bound to Fab, is increased", "why": "Digoxin row."}, {"l": "Opioids: concern in chronic opioid users?", "r": "It can trigger withdrawal", "why": "Opioids row."}], "teach": "Six rows carry a special-considerations question. The other six (calcium channel blockers, tricyclic antidepressants, salicylates, sulfonylureas and meglitinides, iron, organophosphates) have none; on 10/20 she said a row without a question has nothing really weird about it.", "quote": "the column in the table where I ... asked you to answer some questions, I called it special considerations. ... if I didn't have a question, there's nothing really weird about that poisoning. I just was pointing out some ... concerns, some issues with some of these exposures that we need to think about.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf rows ‘Acetaminophen’, ‘Toxic alcohols’, ‘Benzodiazepines’, ‘Beta blockers’, ‘Digoxin’, ‘Opioids’; transcript 10/20"},
{"id": "TBL3-066", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "about", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-key-depth", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "lowYield": true, "stem": "How much detail of the poisoning treatment table is expected on the exam?", "options": [{"t": "The details in the posted key", "correct": true, "why": "Her words on 10/20: what is in her table key is sufficient, and the details in the key must be known."}, {"t": "Everything in the DiPiro chapter and the Marraffa article", "correct": false, "why": "She said students often write more than the key, which is fine, but the key is sufficient."}, {"t": "The antidote names, without their mechanisms", "correct": false, "why": "The key includes mechanisms, and she said its details must be known."}, {"t": "The rows discussed aloud on 10/20", "correct": false, "why": "She did not walk through the rows; she said the key sets the depth."}], "teach": "On 10/20 she said that students often answer in more detail than her key, which is good for learning, but what is in her key is sufficient, and the details in her key must be known. On 10/19 she said toxicology is tested on the board exam and that the class needs to know this table.", "quote": "what I have in my table in the key is sufficient. ... need to know the details that I have in my table, my key.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf; transcript 10/20; transcript 10/19 (board exam)"},
{"id": "TBL3-067", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "about", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-special-empty", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "both", "lowYield": true, "stem": "Several rows of the table have no question in the special-considerations column. What does an empty cell mean?", "options": [{"t": "Nothing is really weird about that poisoning", "correct": true, "why": "Her words on 10/20: if she did not have a question, there is nothing really weird about that poisoning."}, {"t": "The row will not be tested", "correct": false, "why": "She said the details in her key must be known; an empty special column does not remove the row."}, {"t": "The answer was left for the student to look up in the readings", "correct": false, "why": "Her key fills every question she asked; the empty cells had no question."}, {"t": "The poisoning has no antidote", "correct": false, "why": "Every row lists antidotes or treatments."}], "teach": "The special-considerations column holds her questions about concerns and issues with some of the exposures. Where she did not ask a question, there is nothing really weird about that poisoning. The calcium channel blocker, tricyclic antidepressant, salicylate, sulfonylurea, iron and organophosphate rows have no question.", "quote": "the column in the table where I ... asked you to answer some questions, I called it special considerations. ... if I didn't have a question, there's nothing really weird about that poisoning. I just was pointing out some ... concerns, some issues with some of these exposures that we need to think about.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf; transcript 10/20"},
{"id": "TBL3-068", "lecture": "TBL3", "prof": "Smith", "tier": "new", "lowYield": true, "topic": "TBL3", "sub": "match", "skill": "recall", "concept": "tbl-match-reference", "tags": ["smith", "key"], "source": "slide", "type": "match", "stem": "Match each poisoning with the source given in the Reference column of her key.", "left": ["Acetaminophen", "Toxic alcohols", "Benzodiazepines", "Beta blockers", "Calcium channel blockers", "Digoxin", "Tricyclic antidepressants", "Opioids", "Salicylates", "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides", "Iron", "Organophosphate insecticides"], "right": ["Marraffa article", "Basic and Clinical Pharmacology, Chapt 58", "Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 13th Ed. Chapt e6"], "pairs": [{"l": "Acetaminophen", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Acetaminophen row."}, {"l": "Toxic alcohols", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Toxic alcohols row."}, {"l": "Benzodiazepines", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Benzodiazepines row."}, {"l": "Beta blockers", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Beta blockers row."}, {"l": "Calcium channel blockers", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Calcium channel blockers row."}, {"l": "Digoxin", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Digoxin row."}, {"l": "Tricyclic antidepressants", "r": "Basic and Clinical Pharmacology, Chapt 58", "why": "Tricyclic antidepressants row."}, {"l": "Opioids", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Opioids row."}, {"l": "Salicylates", "r": "Basic and Clinical Pharmacology, Chapt 58", "why": "Salicylates row."}, {"l": "Sulfonylureas and meglitinides", "r": "Marraffa article", "why": "Sulfonylureas and miglitinide row."}, {"l": "Iron", "r": "Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 13th Ed. Chapt e6", "why": "Iron row."}, {"l": "Organophosphate insecticides", "r": "Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 13th Ed. Chapt e6", "why": "Organophosphate Insecticides row."}], "teach": "Her key fills the Reference column in all 12 rows. Eight cite the Marraffa article; tricyclic antidepressants and salicylates cite Basic and Clinical Pharmacology, Chapt 58; iron and organophosphate insecticides cite Pharmacotherapy: A Pathophysiologic Approach, 13th Ed. Chapt e6. On 10/20 she asked whether students had filled in that column so they could go back to the source later.", "note": "The key cites Pharmacotherapy Chapt e6; on 10/19 she pointed the class to DiPiro 13th edition chapter E9.", "cite": "PoisoningTreatmentTable2026KEY.pdf, Reference column (all 12 rows)"}
);
